{
  "case_id": "2020-20-18",
  "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:00 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0000-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nJEFFREY L. FISHER, ESQUIRE, Stanford, California; on behalf of the Petitioner.\nSAMUEL T. HARBOURT, Deputy Solicitor General, San Francisco, California; on behalf of the Respondent supporting vacatur.\nAMANDA K. RICE, ESQUIRE, Detroit, Michigan; Court-appointed amicus curiae in support of the judgment below.\nERICA L. ROSS, Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; for the United States, as amicus curiae, supporting affirmance."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Jeffrey L. Fisher",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There is no basis in precedent or history for the categorical pursuit rule applied below. Police officers may not enter a person's home without the approval of a magistrate unless an emergency leaves no time to seek a warrant."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Fisher, you just said that the police could just go ahead and knock on the door. I -- I -- I would expect that would be a terribly dangerous situation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Could we analogize someone who disobeys an officer's orders or resists arrest to an escapee under common law?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice KAGAN: Mr. Fisher, unlike California, you take the position there's no categorical rule even as to fleeing felons. And I'm -- I'm wondering if you would try to defend that for me because I -- I look at our case law and see quite a number of cases which have one after another after another just, you know, said that there is such a rule as to fleeing felons."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Fisher",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Court has had that involves what the parties here would call hot pursuit, and that's Santana. So I don't think all the further -- the more recent references to hot pursuit or to Santana could create a rule that didn't otherwise exist."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Good morning, Mr. Fisher. Just to pick up where Justice Kagan left off, suppose that the hot pursuit doctrine did have a categorical rule for felonies. Would ruling in your favor create kind of an absurd incentive for states to actually make failure to heed a -- a police officer's lights, rather than a misdemeanor, turn it into a felony? Do you worry about that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Constitutional Accountability Center",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0001-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your amicus, the Constitutional Accountability Center, offers more -- seeks to offer more guidance through reference to the common law and identifying the rule that we can draw out of that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, for -- I think, as the Chief Justice mentioned and Justice Breyer, that's a line that we've had quite a bit of difficulty drawing, but beyond that, what"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Harbourt",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your Honor, we think it's pretty speculative to assume that a state would enhance the -- the penal consequences and all of the collateral consequences that come with a felony conviction for -- for certain types of flight merely to circumvent a ruling of this Court limiting the scope of the Hot Pursuit Doctrine."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Harbourt",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your Honor, what I think you do is you recognize --you limit the scope of the hot pursuit exception to felonies. And, of course, that -- that doesn't mean that warrantless entries are not permitted in -- in misdemeanor cases. It just means that the officer in those cases has to identify some case-specific basis to enter the home without -- without first getting a warrant."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Oh, good. What is that? What is that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Not -- not on this case, just in general that the -- you could get into the house based on exigent circumstances without a warrant, correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0002-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And then, on the common law, the amicus at page 23 -- and this is picking up on a question Justice Barrett asked and Justice Thomas asked to Mr. Fisher -- page 23 of the amicus brief says a warrantless arrest at common law could always be made whenever a person was lawfully arrested for any cause and afterwards escaped and sheltered himself in a house. Now, of course, that's after an arrest, but I think Justice Barrett's questions pointed out this is an extremely close analogue in many circumstances. What's your response to that aspect of the common law?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amanda K. Rice",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0002-0010",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Fourth Amendment generally requires officers to get warrants before entering homes. But the warrant requirement is subject to exceptions, and hot pursuit is one of them. The hot pursuit exception justifies warrantless home entry in a narrow class of cases where a suspect tries to thwart a lawful public arrest by outracing an officer to a dwelling. This Court has never suggested that the hot pursuit exception turns on the classification of the underlying offense. It should now expressly hold that it does not. The justifications for the exception relate to the suspect's flight, not the nature of his initial crime."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. RICE: Chief Justice Roberts, the hot pursuit rule serves a crucial law enforcement interest in every case by eliminating perverse incentives for suspects to flee."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE GORSUCH",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 83,
        "start": 83
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Does your rule allow an officer to enter the home for a violation of a regulatory rule as well and, if not, why is drawing that line preferable to drawing the misdemeanor/felony line?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0004-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 84,
        "start": 84
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I guess I'm wondering what the real difference in practice would be between a world where we have a categorical rule and a world where we allow all the Exigent Circumstances Doctrine cases to apply as they exist now."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0004-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 85,
        "start": 85
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: Let me ask you a question now about methodology, a word, the original meaning of the term \"unreasonable\" in the Fourth Amendment. It seems to me that's a different kind of term than \"search\" or \"seizure\" or \"cruel and unusual,\" and \"unreasonable\" means unreasonable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0004-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: Good morning, Mrs. Rice -- Ms. Rice. I have a question about whether -- what it meant to breach the peace, you know, so that seems to be kind of a capacious category, and there's some dispute about what would fall within it. Is there any argument that resisting arrest, that resisting a constable would have been a breach of peace at common law?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0004-0007",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 91,
        "start": 91
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF ERICA L. ROSS FOR THE UNITED STATES, AS AMICUS CURIAE, SUPPORTING AFFIRMANCE"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0004-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 94,
        "start": 94
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice. Just a couple of quick questions, counsel. Did the common law permit entry for -- when there was a -- a case of resisting arrest after a hot pursuit?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0004-0009",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 95,
        "start": 95
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. ROSS: So, Your Honor, I think the issue here is that everyone essentially, I think, acknowledges that the common law does not fully answer this question and does not clearly answer this question."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0004-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 98,
        "start": 98
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Would your rule be different from an across-the-board exigent circumstances rule? Would it apply in felony cases? If IRS officers went to someone's office to arrest a person for tax evasion and the person slipped out and there was a chase, the person went to his house, would it apply -- would it be open to that person to say that -- that -- that you should have gotten -- you needed to get a warrant in that situation?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice KAGAN",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0005-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 103,
        "start": 103
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I want to press on that a little bit, Ms. Ross. I mean, I guess the two questions that I have about this presumption of yours is where does it come from and why is it needed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. ROSS",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0005-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 104,
        "start": 104
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice Kagan, in terms of why we think officers need this presumption, I don't think it's because the -- the end result is going to come out differently. I do think it's an administrability issue. I think in the field it is very helpful for officers to know that, just as they have a conclusive presumption or an absolute rule in the felony context, they generally are going to be able to follow misdemeanor suspects who flee into their homes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice KAVANAUGH",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5b03da1a84ab-0005-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 108,
        "start": 108
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think we have a couple of options here people have explored, but I want to just narrow it down to two, and it's not going to use the word \"presumption.\" But, if -- if I think there are two options here, one is categorical rule for felonies and exigent circumstances for misdemeanor cases."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2020-20-18",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "5b03da1a84ab108d1c96f5f4dc4c7ab6f0203447eca52e5cfd3874c8b4719fd4",
      "evidence_id": "unavailable-5b03da1a84ab-119",
